<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zama Regia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zama Regia was an ancient town in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, a royal residence of the Numidian kings, generally placed at the site of Jama near Siliana. It gave its name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-zama">Battle of Zama</a> of 202 BCE, fought somewhere in the region. In 46 BCE it shut its gates on King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-i">Juba I</a> after his defeat at Thapsus, and it later became a Roman colony.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zama-regia.jpg" alt="Zama Regia" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Panairjdde · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battles_second_punic_war.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
